Here's the full flow in `auth.js` (this is the only file in the repo — nothing else calls into it, so the whole picture is these 40 lines).

## The happy path: `currentToken(store)`

```js
async function currentToken(store) {
  const t = store.get('access');
  if (t && t.expiresAt - Date.now() > SKEW_MS) return t.value;
  return refresh(store);
}
```

It reads the cached access token and checks its `expiresAt` against now, with a 30-second buffer (`SKEW_MS`). If there's more than 30s of life left, it returns the cached value with no network call. Otherwise — token missing, or expiring within 30s — it delegates to `refresh(store)`. So "on a 401" from this function's perspective doesn't really apply: it never makes a request itself, it just decides whether to hand back a cached value or go refresh.

## `refresh(store)`: the actual network call

```js
async function refresh(store) {
  if (inFlight) return inFlight;
  const rt = store.get('refresh');
  if (!rt) throw new Error('no refresh token; re-auth required');
  inFlight = fetch('/oauth/token', { ... })
    .then(async (res) => {
      if (res.status === 401) {
        store.clear();
        throw new Error('refresh rejected; re-auth required');
      }
      if (!res.ok) throw new Error('refresh failed: ' + res.status);
      const body = await res.json();
      store.set('access', { value: body.access_token, expiresAt: Date.now() + body.expires_in * 1000 });
      if (body.refresh_token) store.set('refresh', { value: body.refresh_token });
      return body.access_token;
    })
    .finally(() => { inFlight = null; });
  return inFlight;
}
```

Step by step:
1. If a refresh is already underway (`inFlight` is set), it returns that same promise instead of starting a new one — this is the dedup mechanism, explained below.
2. Otherwise it reads the refresh token from the store. If there isn't one, it throws immediately (as a rejected promise, since this is an `async function`). This throw happens *before* `inFlight` is ever assigned, so nothing gets cached — a second call right after will just fail the same way again rather than reusing anything.
3. Otherwise it POSTs to `/oauth/token` and — critically — assigns the resulting promise chain to the module-level `inFlight` variable synchronously, before any `await` happens.
4. In the response handler: a **401 here means the refresh token itself was rejected** by the auth server (not an expired access token — that's the whole reason we're in this function). It wipes the store completely (both access and refresh tokens) and throws `'refresh rejected; re-auth required'`. There's no retry — the caller is expected to catch this and drive the user back to a login flow.
5. Any other non-OK status throws a generic `'refresh failed: <status>'`.
6. On success, it stores the new access token with a freshly computed `expiresAt`, and — only if the server rotated it — updates the refresh token too (some OAuth servers reuse the same refresh token; others rotate it every time).
7. `.finally` resets `inFlight` to `null` no matter what happened, so the *next* call to `refresh` always starts a clean request.

## The "two requests refresh at once" case

This is the part that matters most before you touch this file. `inFlight` is a single module-level variable, not scoped to `store`, a session, or a request.

Here's why the dedup actually works mechanically: an `async function` body runs synchronously up until its first `await` or `return`. In `refresh`, there's no `await` before the line `inFlight = fetch(...)`. So if two callers both hit `currentToken` around the same time and both decide they need a refresh:

- Caller A calls `refresh(store)`. `inFlight` is `null`, so it proceeds, reads the refresh token, calls `fetch(...)`, and synchronously assigns the resulting `.then().finally()` chain to `inFlight` — all before yielding control back to the event loop.
- Caller B calls `refresh(store)` next (even immediately after, in the same tick). By now `inFlight` is already set, so B just returns that same promise. No second HTTP request goes out.
- Both A and B are now awaiting the exact same promise. When it resolves, both get the same new access token; if it rejects (e.g. 401), both get the same rejection.
- Once settled, `.finally` clears `inFlight`, so the *next* time someone needs a refresh, a fresh request is made rather than replaying a stale result.

The thing to watch out for: this collapsing is global to the module, not per-store. If this code is ever used with more than one `store` in the same process (multiple users/sessions/tenants sharing the module), a refresh triggered for store A would cause a concurrent call for store B to incorrectly receive store A's token/promise, since `refresh` ignores which store triggered the in-flight request. Worth checking whether that's actually possible in your usage before changing anything here — it's the sharpest edge in this file.

## One gap worth knowing about

There's no code here for the "make an API call, get a 401 back because the token was rejected mid-flight, then retry after refreshing" pattern — only for a 401 coming back *from the refresh endpoint itself* (step 4 above). Since nothing else in this repo calls `currentToken`, that request-wrapping/retry-on-401 logic either lives in a caller you haven't shown me, or doesn't exist yet. Worth confirming which, since it changes where you'd hook in a fix.
